Revision history for pg_integrity_checksums (formerly pg_checksums)

Unreleased

    - License: the project is now distributed under the PostgreSQL
      License instead of the MIT License.  Both are permissive; release 2.9.1
      and earlier remain available under the MIT License.
    - Fix an assertion failure in pg_page_checksum_status() and
      pg_relation_page_checksum_status() on a server built with
      --enable-cassert when a block on disk has a corrupt header with
      pd_upper = 0 but is not all zeroes.  The block was reported correctly
      as a mismatch on production builds; the page checksum is now computed
      without calling pg_checksum_page(), which asserts an initialized page.

2.9.1   2026-11-18

    - Performance: on PostgreSQL 17 and later, table, index, bucket and
      database scans read blocks through a read stream, like a sequential
      scan, instead of one ReadBufferExtended() call per block: reads are
      issued ahead, combined into larger I/Os and, on PostgreSQL 18,
      asynchronous.  A parallel scan reads all the block ranges a
      participant claims through one stream, and large relations are handed
      out in larger ranges (up to 8192 blocks, about 2048 ranges per
      relation, as in a parallel sequential scan).  Checksum values do not
      change.  PostgreSQL 12 to 16 read block by block as before.

2.9.0   2026-11-17

    - Fix (changes values): pg_database_logical_checksum() and
      pg_database_physical_checksum() included the tables of
      information_schema even with include_system = false (only pg_catalog
      was left out).  initdb fills them with the server version
      (sql_implementation_info) and the release's features (sql_features),
      so the same data had a different database checksum on clusters of
      different PostgreSQL versions, which defeated comparing a database
      before and after a migration.  information_schema is now a system
      schema like pg_catalog: only include_system = true includes it, in the
      aggregate and the per-relation (pg_database_*_checksums) functions.
      Database checksums computed without include_system change in every
      database; recompute stored values.  Table, index and sequence
      checksums do not change, nor do CLI baselines (the CLI already left
      information_schema out).

    - Feature: the generic (algorithm) functions accept two more algorithms:
      crc32c, a 4-byte CRC-32C computed with PostgreSQL's hardware-
      accelerated implementation, and xxh3, an 8-byte XXH3-64 from the
      vendored xxHash 0.8.3 (third_party/xxhash, BSD-2-Clause).  They
      replace FNV-1a wherever bytes are hashed; the aggregation of rows,
      pages and relations is the same as for fnv32 (crc32c) and fnv64
      (xxh3).  Logical crc32c and xxh3 checksums are portable across
      platforms and clusters like the FNV ones.  For crc32c the seed of
      pg_data_checksum() and pg_text_checksum() is a previous CRC to
      continue.  Every level supports them: data, text, cell, tuple, table,
      index, sequence, database, the per-relation database functions and
      the bucket, row and block range functions.  An unknown algorithm name
      gets a hint listing the supported ones.  Only the function comments change in
      SQL: run ALTER EXTENSION pg_integrity_checksums UPDATE TO '2.9.0'.
      fnv32 and fnv64 values do not change.

    - Feature: pg_table_bucket_checksums() uses parallel workers when
      pg_integrity_checksums.workers is above zero.  Chunks of heap blocks
      are handed out like in a table checksum; each participant keeps its
      own bucket accumulators and adds them to a shared array at the end.
      Temporary tables, and partitions whose columns are in another order
      than the partitioned table's, are still scanned serially.  Results are
      identical to a serial scan; checksum values do not change.
      pg_table_row_checksums() and pg_table_block_checksums() stay serial.

    - Feature: pg_relation_page_checksum_status(relname, fork_name,
      include_ok) verifies every page of a relation against its stored
      pd_checksum in one call; until now pg_page_checksum_status() checked
      one block at a time.  Every fork that exists (main, fsm, vm, init) is
      checked unless fork_name names one.  Each block is read from disk and
      read again before a mismatch is reported, as pg_page_checksum_status()
      does.  Returns the mismatching blocks (fork, blkno, status,
      stored_checksum, calculated_checksum), or every block with status ok,
      mismatch or new when include_ok is true.  Fails when data checksums are
      disabled.  Requires SELECT on the relation.  New SQL function: run
      ALTER EXTENSION pg_integrity_checksums UPDATE TO '2.9.0'.  Checksum
      values do not change.

    - Feature: pg_sequence_logical_checksum(regclass) and the generic
      pg_sequence_logical_checksum(regclass, text) checksum a sequence: its
      data type, start, increment, minimum, maximum, cache and cycle, and
      its last_value and is_called (what pg_dump restores with setval()).
      log_cnt is left out.  Comparing sequences after a migration catches
      rows copied with the sequence left behind, which a table checksum
      cannot see.  The state is read at the call, not as of the snapshot
      (sequences are not transactional), and a physical standby is ahead of
      its primary by the pre-logged values.  Requires SELECT on the
      sequence; returns NULL for an unlogged sequence on a standby.
      Database checksums do not include sequences, so no existing checksum
      value changes.  New SQL functions: run ALTER EXTENSION
      pg_integrity_checksums UPDATE TO '2.9.0'.

    - Fix: pg_index_logical_checksum() read the pages of a table that uses
      another table access method as heap pages; the table checksums already
      rejected such tables.  It now fails with "relation ... does not use
      the heap table access method", like them (and a database checksum
      reports the index as an error, or skips it when strict is off).

    - Fix: a logical index checksum locked the index before its table, the
      reverse of PostgreSQL's own order.  A concurrent DROP INDEX, or DROP
      TABLE of a partition under a partitioned index, deadlocked with it and
      the deadlock detector cancelled one of the two, often the user's DROP.
      pg_index_logical_checksum() and the database checksums (for an index
      computed on its own: parallel scans, or a relations filter) now lock
      the table, and the partitions, first.  Checksum values do not change.

    - Fix: pg_page_checksum_status() reported a modified page of a temporary
      table as mismatch: it compared the local buffer, whose pd_checksum is
      only set when the page is written out.  Temporary tables are now read
      from their file like other relations; a page not written yet is new.
      Temporary tables of other sessions are rejected.

2.8.0   2026-11-17

    - Feature: locate the rows that differ between two copies of a table,
      not only the fact that they differ.  pg_table_bucket_checksums()
      returns a logical checksum per bucket of the key hash space (key: the
      primary key, chosen columns or the whole row); key hashes use the
      canonical encoding, so buckets are comparable between servers.
      pg_table_row_checksums() returns the key, key hash and row checksum of
      the rows in chosen buckets.  pg_table_block_checksums() returns a
      logical checksum per range of heap blocks, for comparing a primary with
      its physical standbys.  With one bucket (or one range covering the
      table) the checksum equals pg_table_logical_checksum.  New SQL
      functions: run ALTER EXTENSION pg_integrity_checksums UPDATE.

    - New: pg_database_logical_checksums() and
      pg_database_physical_checksums() return the checksum of every relation
      of a database scan, one row per relation (relid, schema_name,
      rel_name, relkind, checksum, status, reason), computed in one call with
      one snapshot.  Each checksum equals what the per-relation function with
      the same algorithm returns, so a changed database checksum can be
      traced to the relations that changed.  The scan is the one of the
      aggregate functions: relations are handed to parallel workers, and a
      serial logical scan reads each table once for the table and all of its
      indexes.  An optional relations regclass[] argument limits the scan to
      the listed relations.  In non-strict mode, relations without a usable
      checksum are returned with status skipped or error and a reason.
      Superuser only.  Existing checksum values do not change.  Requires
      ALTER EXTENSION pg_integrity_checksums UPDATE TO '2.8.0'.

    - Fix: on PostgreSQL 17 and later, a parallel database checksum
      (pg_integrity_checksums.workers > 0) called from a statement that
      writes, such as CREATE TABLE AS or INSERT ... SELECT, failed with
      "cannot start commands during a parallel operation" for every relation
      the leader processed: in strict mode the call failed, otherwise those
      relations were skipped with a warning and left out of the result.

    - Fix: columns of type regclass, regtype, regproc, regprocedure, regoper,
      regoperator, regconfig, regdictionary, regnamespace, regrole and
      regcollation were hashed as their OID, so their logical checksum did not
      survive a dump and restore.  They are now hashed by the qualified name
      of the referenced object.  The logical checksum of a table that has
      such a column (including arrays of them) changes; others do not.  A
      plain oid column is still hashed as its number.
    - Fix: tables and materialized views that use a table access method other
      than heap were parsed as heap pages.  The table, tuple and database
      checksum functions now fail with a clear error for them.
    - Fix: pg_integrity_checksums_get_snapshot() tested GetActiveSnapshot()
      for NULL, but that function raises an error instead of returning NULL;
      it now tests ActiveSnapshotSet().

    - Fix: the logical checksum of a partitioned table depended on the column
      order of its partitions.  A partition declaring its columns in another
      order than the parent (for example (b text, a int) under (a int,
      b text)) gave a different checksum than a plain table with the same
      rows.  Leaf partitions are now hashed in the column order of the
      queried table, matched by name.  Checksums of partitioned tables whose
      partitions have another column order change; all others do not.
    - Fix: table and index checksum functions failed on temporary tables
      with pg_integrity_checksums.workers > 0 ("cannot access temporary
      tables during a parallel operation").  Temporary relations are now
      always scanned in the calling backend.
    - Fix: the database checksum functions held a lock on every relation
      until the end of the query, so a database with many relations failed
      with "out of shared memory" (max_locks_per_transaction).  The lock is
      now released as soon as the relation has been processed.

    - Fix: the physical checksum of an index (and of a TOAST table) changed
      after crash recovery although no data changed.  The unused space
      between pd_lower and pd_upper of a page is not guaranteed to be zero:
      it can hold stale bytes left by page compaction, and replaying a
      full-page image zeroes it.  The space is now masked out before
      hashing, so physical checksums no longer depend on it.  Physical
      checksums of indexes and TOAST tables whose pages have such stale
      bytes change once.

    - Fix: logical checksums of text values depended on client_encoding, and
      with parallel workers also on which process read which block: workers
      hashed the text in the server encoding while the leader hashed it in
      the client encoding, so with a client_encoding different from the
      server encoding the same table could give a different checksum on
      every run.  Text types (text, varchar, char(n), name) and the send
      functions of other text-like types (json, xml, ...) are now always
      hashed in the server encoding.  Checksums computed with a
      client_encoding equal to the server encoding (the default) or
      SQL_ASCII do not change; those computed with another client_encoding
      now match them, and no longer fail on characters the client encoding
      cannot represent.

2.7.0   2026-11-15

    - Fix: the logical database checksum summed the per-relation checksums
      without the identity of the relations, so moving rows between two
      tables (or indexes) of the same structure, or swapping their
      contents, did not change it.  Each relation's checksum is now combined
      with a hash of its qualified name (schema.relname); the name, unlike
      the OID, is the same after a dump and restore.  Values of
      pg_database_logical_checksum change for every database, and renaming
      a relation or moving it to another schema now changes them.  Table
      and index logical checksums are not affected.
    - Fix: in a SERIALIZABLE transaction the checksum functions took no
      SIREAD locks and did not check for conflicts with the transactions
      whose tuples they read, so a write skew through a checksum was not
      detected.  Table, index and database scans now lock each table they
      read, like a sequential scan; tuple and cell checksums lock the page.
      New TAP test t/011_serializable.pl (PostgreSQL 16 and later).
    - Fix: physical checksums used pg_class.relfilenode, which is 0 for
      mapped catalogs (pg_class, pg_type, pg_proc, pg_attribute, shared
      catalogs), so a rewrite of such a catalog (VACUUM FULL) did not change
      them.  The relation's actual file number is used now.  Values do not
      change for other relations.
    - Fix: pg_page_checksum_status took a block read from disk with
      pd_upper = 0 for a new page.  Only an all-zero block is new; a
      corrupt header with pd_upper = 0 is now compared like any other page
      and reported as "mismatch".
    - The install and upgrade scripts use CREATE FUNCTION instead of
      CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION, as the PostgreSQL documentation
      recommends for extension scripts: an existing function of the same
      signature is not silently taken over.
    - Documentation: renaming a table or a column does not change its
      logical checksum (the old text said it did); renaming a type does.
    - Code cleanup: 64-bit table and index checksums limit the number of
      workers by the number of blocks like the 32-bit ones; removed
      unreachable NULL-snapshot checks; pg_checksum_bigint no longer
      initialises its result with an enum constant.

    - Fix: the LP_DEAD masking of physical index checksums treated the
      content of every index page between the page header and pd_lower as
      line pointers.  On B-tree and hash metapages, B-tree and GiST deleted
      pages, hash bitmap pages and on all pages of GIN, SP-GiST and BRIN
      indexes that content is something else (metadata, posting lists,
      bitmaps), and bits in it that looked like an LP_DEAD flag were
      cleared, so changes to them did not change the checksum.  LP_DEAD and
      "has garbage" hints are now masked only on B-tree, hash and GiST pages
      that hold index tuples, the only ones index scans set them on.  Values
      change only for indexes that had such a page.
    - Documentation: tuple and table physical checksums include xmin, xmax
      and the command id even without the tuple header, so row locks
      (SELECT ... FOR UPDATE/FOR SHARE, the FOR KEY SHARE lock of foreign
      key checks) and rolled-back UPDATEs/DELETEs change them although no
      data changed.  The regression tests check it.
    - New TAP test t/010_index_metapage.pl.

    - Security: pg_tuple_physical_checksum, pg_tuple_logical_checksum and
      pg_cell_checksum (and their algorithm overloads) did not check tuple
      visibility.  For the TID of a deleted row, an old version of an
      updated row or a row inserted by an uncommitted transaction they
      returned a checksum of data the caller cannot SELECT, and a checksum
      of a short value is easily reversed by trying candidates.  They now
      return NULL for a tuple that is not visible to the query's snapshot.
      Checksums of visible tuples do not change.
    - pg_tuple_physical_checksum returns NULL instead of 0 for a TID without
      a tuple, like the logical tuple and cell functions.
    - Fix: pg_page_checksum_status reads the block from disk without a
      buffer lock, so a read overlapping a concurrent write could see a torn
      page and report a false "mismatch".  A block whose checksum does not
      match is now read again, up to five times, as pg_basebackup does;
      "mismatch" is reported only when every read disagrees.
    - The page checksum TAP test also checks that a corrupted page is
      reported as "mismatch".

    - Fix: logical index checksums with pg_integrity_checksums.workers > 0
      evaluated index expressions and partial-index predicates in parallel
      mode even when they called functions that are not PARALLEL SAFE (the
      default for user-defined functions), which fails or misbehaves (for
      example "cannot be set during a parallel operation").  Such indexes
      are now computed without workers; a parallel database checksum
      computes them in the leader after the parallel part.  Values do not
      change.
    - Fix: a relation dropped while a database checksum was running made the
      checksum fail with "could not open relation with OID", and in strict
      mode (the default) aborted it, so a routine DROP TABLE in a busy
      database made scheduled checks fail at random.  Each relation is now
      locked and looked up before it is read; one that no longer exists is
      left out with a NOTICE, in strict mode too, since it is no longer part
      of the database.

    - Security: logical index checksums (pg_index_logical_checksum and
      pg_database_logical_checksum with include_indexes, the default)
      evaluated index expressions and partial-index predicates with the
      privileges of the caller.  A role that could create a table and an
      index could therefore run arbitrary SQL as the superuser computing a
      database checksum, or as any role computing an index checksum (for
      example through the CLI).  The index definition is now evaluated as
      the owner of the table in a security-restricted operation, with a GUC
      nest level that undoes SET commands, and on PostgreSQL 17 and later
      with a restricted search_path, as REINDEX, ANALYZE and amcheck do.
      Checksum values do not change.
    - Fix: logical checksums depended on quote_all_identifiers, which
      pg_dump sets and any session can change.  Type tags were built with
      quote_identifier() and format_type_be(), which quote every identifier
      when the setting is on, so a session with it on computed different
      values for columns of types such as jsonb, inet or user-defined types,
      and a session that changed it mid-way kept or lost the difference
      depending on what the type cache already held.  Tags are now always
      built as with the default (off), so stored checksums stay valid.

    - Fix: parallel table and index checksums used the snapshot restored in
      a worker without making it active.  PostgreSQL builds with assertions
      (--enable-cassert) aborted the worker; release builds were not
      affected.  The worker now makes the snapshot active, as the database
      scan already did.
    - Fix: pg_page_checksum and pg_page_checksum_status read the page from
      disk into a buffer that was not I/O-aligned, which PostgreSQL 16 and
      later require (an assertion failure in assert-enabled builds, and a
      problem with debug_io_direct).
    - Performance: logical checksums are 2-5x faster and every checksum
      value is unchanged.  Measured with bench/ on PostgreSQL 19devel, data
      in shared buffers, no parallel workers: logical table checksums of a
      1M-row table 354 -> 118 ms, of a 60-column table 538 -> 108 ms,
      logical index checksums up to 2.2x, pg_database_logical_checksum
      2018 -> 801 ms.  With 4 workers the gains are the same.
    - The type cache keeps the FmgrInfo of send and output functions, so a
      value no longer costs a pg_proc lookup.  Cache entries stay in place
      across invalidations (a generation counter marks them out of date), and
      the cache is also invalidated by pg_proc changes.
    - The common built-in types (bool, "char", int2/4/8, oid, date, time,
      timetz, timestamp(tz), money, float4/8, interval, uuid, text, varchar,
      bpchar, name, bytea) are encoded without calling the send function and
      without its 1 kB buffer.  The bytes hashed are exactly what the send
      function returns; text types still go through textsend() when it would
      convert to a different client encoding.
    - Rows are deformed once instead of with heap_getattr() per column
      (quadratic in the number of columns), the type tag and column position
      are hashed once per column instead of once per value, and the FNV-1a
      primitives are inlined.
    - A serial pg_database_logical_checksum reads each table once for the
      table and all of its indexes instead of once per relation.  If the
      combined pass fails, the relations are computed one by one as before,
      so warnings, strict-mode errors and the skipped count do not change.
      Parallel database scans keep the per-relation split, which balances
      better across workers.
    - Heap scans skip per-tuple visibility checks on all-visible pages (as a
      sequential scan does), and physical index and TOAST pages are hashed
      after the buffer lock is released, from the masked copy.
    - Add bench/, a benchmark that loads a data set and reports the median
      time and the value of each checksum, for comparing two builds.
    - Add regression tests checksum_fastpath (the direct encodings against
      the real send functions, including another client encoding) and
      checksum_dbgroup (the grouped database scan and its fallback).

2.6.0   2026-11-14

    - Fix: physical index checksums no longer change when nothing changed.
      Index pages were hashed as raw buffer bytes, including the page LSN,
      the in-buffer pd_checksum (stale until the page is evicted, because
      PostgreSQL sets the checksum on the copy it writes out) and the hints
      index scans set under a share lock (LP_DEAD line pointers, B-tree
      BTP_HAS_GARBAGE, hash LH_PAGE_HAS_DEAD_TUPLES, GiST F_HAS_GARBAGE).  A
      plain SELECT or a server restart therefore changed the result.  Pages
      are now hashed from a copy with that state masked out.
    - Fix: physical TOAST checksums (pg_table_physical_checksum on a TOAST
      relation, database physical checksums with include_toast) mask the same
      page state and the infomask hint bits of TOAST tuples.
    - Value change: physical checksums of indexes and TOAST relations differ
      from 2.5.0.  Re-capture physical baselines after upgrading.  Logical
      checksums and physical heap checksums are unchanged.
    - Fix: logical table, tuple, cell and index checksums no longer
      canonicalize values under a buffer content lock.  Detoasting, type send
      functions and index expressions ran with interrupts held off, so a slow
      expression or a large toasted value could not be cancelled
      (statement_timeout and pg_cancel_backend waited for it to finish), and
      reading other buffers and the catalogs under the lock risked an
      undetectable LWLock deadlock.  Visibility is still checked under the
      lock; hashing now runs with the buffer only pinned.
    - Add regression tests checksum_physical_hints and checksum_buffer_locks
      and TAP test 009_physical_restart.
    - The pure utility functions are IMMUTABLE and PARALLEL SAFE:
      pg_data_checksum and pg_text_checksum (both overloads) and
      pg_checksum_bigint.  The algorithm overloads and pg_checksum_bigint
      used to be VOLATILE, and none was PARALLEL SAFE, so they could not be
      used in index expressions or generated columns.
    - Build: drop the PostgreSQL 19 version gates.  Arrays are decomposed
      with deconstruct_array() instead of the array_iter API, and the
      pg_class scan of database checksums uses systable_beginscan(); both
      have the same signature on every supported release, so the extension
      also builds against PostgreSQL 19 development snapshots from before
      the API changes.  Checksum values are unchanged.
    - Docs: the fnv64 NULL sentinel is 0x00000000FFFFFFFF (the 32-bit
      sentinel zero-extended), not UINT64_MAX as documented.  The value is
      unchanged.
    - Add 2.5.0 -> 2.6.0 upgrade script and the checksum_upgrade regression
      test, which installs 2.0.0, updates to the default version and checks
      that every function matches a fresh install (attributes, privileges
      and comments).

2.5.0   2026-11-12

    - Behavior change: pg_integrity_checksums.strict now defaults to on.  A
      database checksum (pg_database_physical_checksum and
      pg_database_logical_checksum, with or without an algorithm argument)
      fails when a relation cannot be checksummed, for example an invalid
      index, an unsupported relation kind or a scan error.  Previously such
      a relation was skipped with a WARNING, so a corrupt table did not show
      up as a changed result.
    - To keep the old behavior set pg_integrity_checksums.strict = off, per
      session, per role or database (ALTER ROLE/DATABASE ... SET) or in
      postgresql.conf.
    - No checksum values change.
    - With strict mode off, the summary WARNING now says how many relations
      were skipped (invalid indexes and unsupported index access methods are
      counted too; they used to be skipped silently), including in parallel
      scans.
    - Errors raised in strict mode now name the relation (a CONTEXT line:
      "while computing the database checksum of relation ..."), and the
      invalid-index error says that the index is invalid.
    - Add 2.4.0 -> 2.5.0 upgrade script (no SQL object changes), a regression
      test for the strict default across all database-level functions and
      parallel scans, and a TAP test for corrupt pages and unlogged tables on a
      standby.

2.4.0   2026-11-12

    - Fix: logical checksums no longer depend on dropped columns.  Table
      columns, composite fields and pg_cell_checksum() now hash the position
      among the live columns instead of the physical attribute number, so a
      table with dropped columns has the same logical checksum as an
      identical table without them (for example after pg_dump/restore).
      Tables without dropped columns are unchanged.
    - Fix: logical checksums no longer depend on the session's search_path.
      User-defined types are identified by their unqualified name instead of
      format_type_be(), which qualified the name when the schema was not on
      the search_path.  Types in pg_catalog and types visible in the default
      search_path are unchanged.
    - Fix: physical tuple checksums that include the tuple header
      (include_header = true) ignore the xmin/xmax hint bits, which readers
      set lazily; the value used to change after the first read of a tuple.
      Checksums without the header are unchanged.
    - Revoke EXECUTE from PUBLIC on the generic database-level overloads
      (pg_database_*_checksum(boolean, boolean, text)); they already required
      a superuser.
    - Add 2.3.0 -> 2.4.0 upgrade script and a regression test for the
      portability fixes.

    Baselines are affected only if they were taken (1) on tables with dropped
    columns or composite types with dropped attributes, (2) on user-defined
    types from a session whose search_path did not include the type's schema,
    or (3) with include_header = true.  See "Upgrading to 2.4" in
    docs/installation.md.

2.3.0   2026-11-10

    - Add pg_page_checksum_status(regclass, integer) to verify a page against
      its stored pd_checksum, including data_checksums-disabled reporting.
    - Clarify that pg_page_checksum() calculates a 16-bit page checksum and
      does not itself verify the stored value.

2.2.0   2026-11-09

    - Add generic algorithm overloads for data, text, cell, tuple, table,
      index and database logical checksums, plus physical tuple, table,
      index and database checksums.
    - Add FNV-1a 64-bit digests and the pg_checksum_bigint conversion helper.
    - Add parallel execution for 64-bit checksums; the 64-bit database path
      also reuses the existing parallel worker pool.
    - Add upgrade script from 2.1.0 to 2.2.0.

2.1.0   2026-10-31

    - Make logical checksums independent of the server's native endianness.
      Array dimensions/lower bounds, composite field counts and multirange
      counts are now hashed in a canonical little-endian byte order instead of
      hashing native int/uint32 bytes.
    - Logical checksum values on little-endian systems are unchanged; 2.x
      baselines remain valid.  Big-endian systems now produce the same logical
      values as little-endian systems.
    - Physical checksums remain storage- and architecture-dependent by design.
    - Add a manually triggered FreeBSD powerpc64 big-endian test workflow.
    - No SQL object changes; the 2.0.2 -> 2.1.0 upgrade script is a no-op.

2.0.2   2026-10-29

    - Build against PostgreSQL 19 stable: adapt to the changed array_iter_setup,
      array_iter_next and table_beginscan APIs.
    - Add PostgreSQL 19 to CI, ASAN/UBSAN and coverage workflows,
      cross-version logical baselines, and property/fuzz tests.
    - No SQL object changes; the 2.0.1 -> 2.0.2 upgrade script is a no-op.

2.0.1   2026-10-29

    - Fix logical checksums of system catalogs containing anyarray values
      (notably pg_statistic): the relation was skipped with "no binary output
      function available for type aclitem" when system catalogs were included.
    - Fix logical index checksums for Hash indexes.  Hash index descriptors
      store an int4 hash code, but FormIndexDatum returns the original key;
      logical checksums now use the original input type, so a Hash index and a
      B-tree index on the same column have the same logical content and match
      across databases.
    - Add extensive type, system-catalog, DDL, privilege, TOAST, error and
      cross-database tests.
    - Hash-index logical checksums change because the previous values used
      the index descriptor's int4 type for the original key value.  Recompute
      index and database logical baselines that include Hash indexes.
    - Database logical checksums with include_system=true change because
      system catalogs such as pg_statistic are now covered instead of skipped.
    - No SQL object changes; the 2.0.0 -> 2.0.1 upgrade script is a no-op.

2.0.0   2026-10-28

    - BREAKING: rename the extension from pg_checksums to
      pg_integrity_checksums.  The new name avoids confusion with the
      pg_checksums utility shipped with PostgreSQL core and with the unrelated
      credativ pg_checksums_ext tool.
    - BREAKING: rename the GUC prefix from pg_checksums.* to
      pg_integrity_checksums.*.
    - SQL function names and checksum values are unchanged.  Existing
      checksum baselines remain valid after reinstalling the extension.
    - There is no ALTER EXTENSION rename path.  Migrate with:
      DROP EXTENSION pg_checksums;
      CREATE EXTENSION pg_integrity_checksums;
      then update pg_checksums.workers/strict settings to the new prefix.
    - The 1.3.x extension remains available from the previous PGXN release and
      git tag for users who need the old name.

1.3.2   2026-10-28

    - Performance-only release: all checksum values and SQL APIs are
      unchanged.
    - Cache base type, element type and type I/O metadata once per type
      instead of performing syscache lookups for every cell.
    - Iterate array elements with array_iter (no intermediate Datum/bool
      arrays) and keep small composite arguments on the stack.
    - Avoid duplicate page and tuple validation during physical table scans.
    - Reuse one BufferAccessStrategy per scan worker, scan 64 blocks per
      atomic work item, and do not start more workers than there are chunks.

1.3.1   2026-10-24

    - Parallelize pg_index_logical_checksum(): the heap scan and index
      definition evaluation are split across pg_checksums.workers.  Serial and
      parallel results are identical.  Partitioned indexes aggregate their
      leaf indexes as before.
    - Remove unused parallel-scan state fields and the obsolete logical-table
      found_invalid flag.

1.3.0   2026-10-23

    - BREAKING: replace the legacy logical checksum format.  Logical
      checksums are now canonical, 32-bit values that exclude relation OIDs,
      heap TIDs and type OIDs.  Enum labels, numeric scale, arrays,
      composites, domains and ranges are encoded canonically, so identical
      table content can be compared across databases (same major version and
      compatible schema/type names).
    - BREAKING: pg_table_logical_checksum() no longer requires a primary key
      and no longer mixes the relation OID into the result.  Duplicate rows are
      preserved by a sum-plus-count aggregate.  Partitioned tables accumulate
      all leaf rows into the same accumulator.
    - BREAKING: pg_tuple_logical_checksum() loses the include_header argument.
      The tuple header is never part of a logical checksum.
    - BREAKING: pg_index_logical_checksum() is now derived from the visible
      table rows and the index definition instead of B-tree pages.  It works
      for every index access method, supports expression, partial, INCLUDE and
      partitioned indexes, and contains no heap TIDs or dead entries.  A
      physical index checksum remains the tool for detecting missing/corrupt
      index entries.
    - BREAKING: pg_database_logical_checksum(include_system, include_indexes)
      replaces the old (include_system, include_toast) signature.  It includes
      tables without primary keys and optionally logical index checksums; TOAST
      content is covered through the parent table.
    - Remove pg_table_content_checksum(); pg_table_logical_checksum() now
      provides the primary-key-free content checksum.
    - Rework table and database logical aggregates for the canonical format,
      including parallel workers.  pg_data_checksum() and pg_text_checksum()
      remain low-level utilities.
    - Physical checksums are unchanged.  All logical baselines must be
      recomputed.

1.2.0   2026-10-18

    - Add partitioned index support.  pg_index_physical_checksum() and
      pg_index_logical_checksum() now aggregate the checksums of all leaf
      indexes instead of returning NULL for the partitioned parent.  Each leaf
      contributes combine(leaf checksum, leaf OID) to an order-independent
      aggregate, and the parent OID is folded in at the end; logical
      aggregation does the same for B-tree leaves.
    - Add pg_table_content_checksum(regclass).  It does not require a primary
      key and does not include relation OIDs, so it can be used for tables
      without a PK and for content comparison across databases.  Duplicate
      rows are preserved by a sum-plus-count aggregate, and partitioned tables
      aggregate all leaf rows into the same accumulator.
    - Add the pg_checksums.strict GUC.  When enabled, database checksum
      functions fail if a relation cannot be checksummed (missing primary key,
      unsupported index access method, or a scan error) instead of silently
      skipping it.  The default remains false.  Strict errors release the
      per-relation internal subtransaction before propagating, so an exception
      handler can catch them safely.
    - Fix parallel scans to destroy the parallel context and leave parallel
      mode when a worker or the leader fails.  Previously an error caught by a
      PL/pgSQL exception block could leave the backend in parallel mode, making
      later RESET or nested parallel operations fail.
    - Existing checksum values are unchanged for non-partitioned relations;
      partitioned indexes change from NULL to an aggregated value, and the new
      content checksum has its own algorithm.

1.1.1   2026-10-18

    - Security: enforce SELECT privilege (and reject relations protected by
      row-level security) in pg_page_checksum(), pg_cell_checksum(),
      pg_tuple_*_checksum(), pg_table_*_checksum() and pg_index_*_checksum().
      Previously any user could compute checksums of any relation, including
      tables they could not read.
    - Security: the database-level functions are no longer SECURITY DEFINER.
      With SECURITY DEFINER, superuser() saw the owner and the superuser check
      could be bypassed by any user.  EXECUTE is now revoked from PUBLIC, and
      the extension can no longer be installed by a non-superuser.
    - Fix a server crash (signal 11) when pg_index_logical_checksum() was
      called on an SP-GiST index.  Logical index checksums are now B-tree
      only; Hash, GiST, GIN, SP-GiST and BRIN return NULL instead of
      returning a meaningless value.  Physical index checksums remain
      available for all supported access methods.
    - pg_page_checksum() now validates the block number.  A negative block
      number used to be interpreted as P_NEW and silently extended the
      relation; an out-of-range block is now an error.  The page is copied
      before pg_checksum_page() is called, so the shared pd_checksum field is
      never transiently zeroed in the buffer pool.
    - Validate relkinds, line pointers, tuple headers and page headers before
      following them.  A corrupted page or a bogus TID now produces a clean
      error instead of reading out of bounds.  pg_cell_checksum() and
      pg_tuple_logical_checksum() no longer read past PageGetMaxOffsetNumber.
    - Fix PG_RETURN_NULL() inside PG_TRY and mark locals modified between
      setjmp and longjmp as volatile in the tuple checksum functions.
    - Database scans now run each relation in an internal subtransaction.
      Errors release locks and buffer pins, are reported as a warning and no
      longer produce a silently partial checksum.  Cancellation and statement
      timeouts are propagated instead of being swallowed.  PostgreSQL 12-16
      prohibit internal subtransactions in parallel mode; there a relation
      error aborts a parallel database scan, while serial scans (workers = 0)
      and PostgreSQL 17+ still skip the relation with a warning.
    - Enter parallel mode before creating a parallel context and leave it
      after destroying it.  This fixes assertion failures in --enable-cassert
      builds and prevents the leader from starting nested parallel workers
      during a database scan.  Workers are also clamped by
      max_worker_processes, and the block-chunk end calculation no longer
      overflows.
    - Physical checksums of TOAST relations now hash their raw pages instead
      of misinterpreting toast tuples as heap tuples.  include_toast works
      independently of include_system, and logical database checksums skip
      TOAST relations (their content is covered by detoasting the parent
      table).  Shared catalogs are excluded from per-database checksums even
      when include_system is true.
    - Physical table checksums no longer include relpages/reltuples.  Those
      are planner statistics, not physical data: ANALYZE could change a
      physical checksum without any data change, and the implicit float4 to
      uint32 conversion was undefined for reltuples = -1.  Physical table and
      database checksums computed with 1.1.0 must be refreshed.
    - Tuple and table physical checksums no longer include the page LSN,
      pd_checksum and pd_flags.  Hint-bit-only changes made by a concurrent
      scan could change those fields, so serial and parallel scans of the same
      table could disagree.  They now include the relation's relfilenode and
      reltablespace, so VACUUM FULL, CLUSTER, REINDEX and SET TABLESPACE still
      change physical checksums.  Page-level checksums still hash the raw
      page bytes.
    - Skip unsupported index access methods in physical database scans
      instead of silently folding a zero checksum into the aggregate.
    - pg_index_physical_checksum() and pg_index_logical_checksum() return NULL
      for partitioned indexes until leaf-index aggregation is implemented.
    - Logical checksums are unchanged for the same data.  Physical checksums
      have intentionally changed: planner statistics and transient page
      metadata were removed, and relfilenode/reltablespace were added.  Any
      physical baseline computed with 1.1.0 must be refreshed.  Database
      checksums can also change when include_system/include_toast is used
      (shared catalogs and unsupported indexes are now handled correctly) or
      when a database contains non-B-tree indexes.
    - TAP tests are opt-in again (enable_tap_tests=yes), matching the README,
      and run_tests.sh no longer stops a server it did not start and no longer
      requires sudo unconditionally.

1.1.0   2026-10-17

    - Speed up the logical checksum paths considerably. The per-cell pg_type
      system cache lookup is gone, the primary key is looked up once per
      relation instead of once per tuple, and short-header varlenas are hashed
      in place instead of being detoasted into a fresh copy for every cell.
      Logical table checksums are up to ~180x faster on large tables; physical
      and utility functions are unchanged.
    - Fix a relcache reference leak in the parallel database scan: parallel
      workers cannot read the session's temporary tables, and the error that
      this raised left the relation open.
    - Database checksums no longer include temporary relations. They are
      session-local, and including them made the parallel result depend on how
      the work happened to be distributed. Checksums of persistent objects are
      unchanged, and the parallel and serial results now agree.
    - Initialise the relation and buffer variables used by the exception
      handler of pg_tuple_physical_checksum.
    - ALTER EXTENSION pg_checksums UPDATE TO '1.1.0' is a no-op: the SQL
      objects are unchanged.

1.0.0   2026-10-12

    - First public release on PGXN.
    - Checksum functions at six levels: page, cell, tuple, table, index and
      database, in physical and logical variants (cell level is logical only).
    - FNV-1a 32-bit implementation, with order-independent aggregation so that
      table, index and database checksums do not depend on scan order.
    - Parallel scans for table, index and database checksums, controlled by the
      pg_checksums.workers GUC.
    - Partitioned tables are aggregated over all leaf partitions.
    - Supports PostgreSQL 12, 13, 14, 15, 16, 17 and 18.
